## Make room for a reply

In a support thread, three lines are enough for a quick thank-you, but not for a description of a billing error. Give the textarea a visible label linked to its `id`, the class `reply`, and `rows="3"`. That row count sets the starting height for the ordinary textarea. With content sizing active, `min-block-size` sets the starting height instead.

```css
.reply {
  box-sizing: content-box;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  border: 1px solid;
  inline-size: calc(100% - 1rem - 2px);
  line-height: 1.5;
}

@supports (field-sizing: content) {
  .reply {
    field-sizing: content;
    min-block-size: 3lh;
    max-block-size: 8lh;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }
}
```

The inline size leaves room for both padding and border, so the field fits its container and text wraps rather than widening it. The `lh` unit follows the textarea’s own line height. With `content-box`, all eight line heights are available for text; padding and border sit outside that limit.

## Check both directions

Paste a paragraph that wraps past eight lines. Look for the field to grow until line eight, then scroll internally without moving the submit button farther down. Delete the paragraph a sentence at a time: the field should shrink back to three lines.

